1. Which model handled requirement changes more effectively?
Scrum handled changes better because new requirements were added to the backlog without affecting ongoing sprint work.

2. Which approach required more upfront planning?
Waterfall (Kanban) required more upfront planning since requirements must be defined clearly at the beginning.

3. How did Taiga features differ for Kanban and Scrum projects?
Kanban: Focuses on continuous flow using boards
Scrum: Uses backlog, sprints, story points, and milestones

4. Which model is more suitable for real-world software projects and why?
 Scrum is more suitable because real-world projects often have changing requirements and need continuous feedback and incremental delivery.
